<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct an ablation study on MM-FakeBench using the GPT-4.1-nano backbone over 1,000 samples to evaluate the effectiveness of the multi-source verification MCTS and toolset in our framework. Results are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ T2Agent: A Tool-augmented Multimodal Misinformation Detection Agent with Monte Carlo Tree Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MCTS search enables flexible evidence verification.</span>
MMD-Agent follows a rigid, rule-based verification path with fixed state transitions, limiting its adaptability when handling incomplete or low-confidence evidence across diverse forgery sources. In contrast, introducing MCTS-based tree search allows our agent to dynamically explore and prioritize subtasks based on real-time confidence estimates. This flexibility leads to notable improvements across all metrics: the F1 score increases from 0.398 to 0.535 (+34.4%) and accuracy from 0.424 to 0.534 (+25.9%). The most significant gain is observed in recall (+49.2%), indicating that MCTS enhances the model’s ability to uncover true positive cases by exploring multiple forgery sources and avoiding premature termination.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Extensible tools enhance performance through adaptive reasoning.</span>
In the basic MCTS setting, only fundamental tools such as image understanding and Wikipedia are utilized. To further improve performance, we optimized and finally selected a subset of additional tools, including Google Search and entity detection via Baidu API <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            https://ai.baidu.com/tech/imagerecognition/general</span></span></span>. These tools enable more comprehensive information retrieval and fine-grained object classification, respectively. For example, Google Search provides access to broader contextual data and entity detection helps identify detailed categories of objects within images.
The integration of these extensible tools into the MCTS framework significantly boosts overall performance. Our method achieves an F1 score of 0.568 and an accuracy of 0.569, representing relative gains of +6.2% and +6.5%, respectively, over the MCTS-only setup. Notably, precision rises to 0.564 (+5.4% over MCTS alone), reflecting improved reliability in final judgments due to more accurate evidence validation via selected tools.</p>
</div>
