<td id="A1.T6.2.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2.28</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost analysis</span>
Table <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ A.5 More Analysis ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ T2Agent: A Tool-augmented Multimodal Misinformation Detection Agent with Monte Carlo Tree Search" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> compares the inference costs (in USD) between the MMD-Agent and our method on the MMFakeBench dataset, using two GPT-4 variants: GPT-4o and GPT-4.1-nano. As shown, our method has higher computational costs under both model settings.
This increased cost primarily stems from the more thorough exploration strategy employed by our approach. Unlike the MMD-Agent, which follows a fixed sequential verification process, our method dynamically explores multiple forgery sources and incorporates confidence-based decision making. This leads to more frequent interactions with the LVLMs, thereby increasing the overall inference cost.
While our method has higher computational costs, it delivers significantly better performance. Future work may focus on optimizing the exploration strategy to reduce cost while preserving high accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Cost comparison (USD) between MMD-agent and our T<sup id="A1.T7.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Agent  on MMfakebench.</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T7.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T7.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
